#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115668 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "64b2036f-da0f-59de-8a39-6b33e6cbdd04" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a True Statement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a53765dd-7014-5b39-bf54-e8f3976b2384" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113409} {:answer/id #uuid "13322188-8978-5455-b6c9-479a518c6596" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113410} {:answer/id #uuid "22096abe-1943-50f3-99f0-aa1dafa8ad8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113411} {:answer/id #uuid "4ca7fc4f-bcc4-5cd1-80f3-0af1032a7077" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113412} {:answer/id #uuid "55d76481-214e-51c0-a915-5a564235ade6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113413}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13322188-8978-5455-b6c9-479a518c6596" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113410} :answer-field/id #uuid "55d8db78-0edf-543a-9a4c-fbbba37ddad1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113408}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e42babae-3f60-5ad6-9c62-eaf91af5f106" :question/math-academy-id "q-32167" :question/problem "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n*If a $2\\times 2$-matrix $A$ is invertible, then $\\text{__________}$ . *\n\n- $Ax=0$ has exactly one solution\n- $Ax=0$ has infinitely many solutions\n- $det(A)\\ne 0$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nNow, since the first part of the invertible matrix theorem is true ($A$ is invertible), all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only options I and III give us true statements:\n\n- *If $A$ is invertible, then $Ax=0$ has exactly one solution.* $\\,✓$\n\n- *If $A$ is invertible, then $det(A)\\ne 0.$* $\\,✓$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351565 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "64b2036f-da0f-59de-8a39-6b33e6cbdd04" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a True Statement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11e2ddf0-f804-5a30-980d-66304828d094" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}]=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351567} {:answer/id #uuid "5be870e4-e9bd-5937-987a-d79308c5cc3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has no solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351568} {:answer/id #uuid "4a2b59cd-65d9-5d5c-8608-f9aa1b253dd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The lines $x+y=0$ and $x+ky=0$ are parallel" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351569} {:answer/id #uuid "81b9a644-f5fb-5579-a1e9-3121dea65ebc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]$ is a solution for the system $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351570} {:answer/id #uuid "bca0fdcf-aa0b-5275-8400-08e14037c3b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The system $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has the unique zero solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351571}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bca0fdcf-aa0b-5275-8400-08e14037c3b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The system $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has the unique zero solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351571} :answer-field/id #uuid "266ea60b-4a4f-549a-9d0f-158bc22cdf50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351566}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0b78864d-df1c-54a9-a660-cac490a85eb8" :question/math-academy-id "q-73608" :question/problem "Consider the lines $x+y=0$ and $x+ky=0$, where $k$ is a constant. Complete the following statement so that it is true:\n\n*If $k\\ne 1$, then $\\text{__________}\\,.$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nLet $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}].$ If $k\\ne 1$, then\n\n$$\ndet(A)=det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}]=1-k\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nSo, according to the invertible matrix theorem $A$ is invertible and $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ is the unique solution of  $Ax=0.$\nAlso, the system \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \nhas exactly one solution, and the lines $x+y=0$ and $x+ky=0$ intersect at exactly one point. \n\nTherefore, the true statement is as follows:\n\n*If $k\\ne 1$, then the system $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has the unique zero solution.* $\\,✓$"}]}
